// Whom the owner's post is for, when several agents take part in its ticket and the words name nobody (design §13):
// base.kind's owner_post handler makes the step, owed by the watcher, which coordd answers itself as it does intake.
// It runs `whom` below once, statelessly, with the model policy names for the task `route`, and answers `route` with
// its verdict (design §13, second words: ordered rules, the agent typed to is the default). whom's TO is route's,
// written out by coordd ("lead"), or "nobody (it was posted on the ticket itself)"; AGENTS is every agent on the
// ticket, TO and OTHERS in one list; RECENT is coordd's: the ticket's last three posts before this one, each cut
// short, with whom each went to. A call that fails, answers out of shape, gives nobody, or names an agent not on the
// ticket is answered To(to) when the words were typed to someone; typed to nobody (the owner's own post on the
// ticket), To the agent the ticket is for (its form's `to`), when it is on the ticket, else where his last post on it
// went (those of them still on it; else an earlier post's: consult-3 N-1446 p110), so his post goes and no press comes
// back to him (proto-lead N-1446 p57.2); Unclear("the call that says whom it is for failed") only when
// there is neither, so the owner, who has just posted, picks and nothing sits unheard (paper-research m1341).
//
// The thread's holder (the owner's fundamentals, N-1104 p593-p606; consult-3 N-1443 p2, 3: `route` is where the
// holder for his posts starts): one fresh call that is given the thread's report and answers one form. Besides RECENT,
// coordd writes THREAD, what the thread is and whom it was opened for, with what each agent on it has been given
// there and not yet answered, and YOURS, his posts on it since an agent last posted there, each with where it went
// (not the words an agent opened the ticket with, which thread.kind posts again as his: N-1445 #2, which RECENT lists
// under that agent; the machine's `his_words`, proto-lead N-1446 p83, p105); all of it is the
// record's (its posts, `told` and `given` lines), so a replay reads the same. Rule 5's "the post that opened the
// ticket" is proto-reader's (N-1446 p71): N-1445 #3 answers paper-research's opener and is for paper-research-brain. JOINS in the answer:
// the earlier post of his this one continues (the same thought sent in two goes; his p586 and p587 on 20 Nov, 22 s
// apart), one of YOURS; coordd leaves it out when it is none of them. It is on the `answer` line, so the record and
// the window show what the holder decided. A call or answer from before JOINS reads as it did: a pattern names the
// first fields only, and a trailing optional field may be left out.
//
// His burst (E1b; the owner, N-1445 p9: "hold them for 15s by default and ask a docket program to split them"):
// base.kind's `gather` holds his typed posts together, so TEXT may be several posts, which coordd writes each with its
// number ("#590: …"). SPLIT: its posts are for different agents (paper-research N-1446 p40: one about the build, one
// about the brain), each part whole posts and whom they go to (proto-lead p61.5: a part never cuts a post; cutting
// one into passages is N-696's `split`, split.rs). coordd takes a Split only when every post of the burst is in
// exactly one part and each part goes to agents on the ticket; of one post, a Split is its one part's To. A post of
// the burst that carries his own choice (its @NAMEs, the post it answers, `@all`), when they do not all carry one same,
// is written "#591 (his own choice: it goes to docket-core and no one else): …", and coordd takes an answer, To or
// Split, only when the part holding it goes to exactly those agents (E1b hold 1: proto-reader N-1446 p307, proto-lead
// p325); else, or when the call fails, each such post goes where he chose and the others as above. An Unclear is
// taken: base.kind sends each such post where he chose and asks him about the others only; when every post carries a
// choice, no call is made (proto-reader p445, consult-3 p446).

/// whole posts of his burst, and whom they go to
type Part {
  posts: [Int]
  to: [Agent]
}

call route(text: Text, to: [Agent], others: [Agent]) -> To(agents: [Agent], joins: Int?) | Split(parts: [Part]) | Unclear(why: Text)
  task route

call whom(text: Text, to: Text, agents: Text, recent: Text, thread: Text, yours: Text) -> To(agents: [Agent], joins: Int?) | Split(parts: [Part]) | Unclear(why: Text)
  task route
  template
    The owner of a software team wrote the message below on a ticket that several agents take part in. You hold this
    ticket for him: say which of them is handed it now; the others see it at their next turn and are not woken. You do
    not decide who does the work, and you do not judge the message: an agent handed a vague message asks the owner
    about it itself.
    It was typed to: {{ to }}
    The ticket: {{ thread }}
    His posts on it just before this one, since an agent last posted there, with where each went:
    {{ yours }}
    Take the first rule that fits:
    1. The words begin with the name of an agent on the ticket, or speak to one of them ("fixer, please also ..."):
       To that agent.
    2. The words ask every agent ("what do you all think", "everyone stop"): To all of them.
    3. It was typed to an agent: To that agent. A short answer ("yes", "go ahead", "no, keep it") typed to an agent is
       for that agent.
    4. It was typed to nobody and goes on from a post of his just before it (the same thought, sent a moment later):
       To the agent that post went to.
    5. It was typed to nobody: To the agent whose last post it answers; when it answers the post that opened the
       ticket, or no agent's post: the agent the ticket was opened for. Only if you cannot tell which agent that is:
       Unclear(why), with one sentence saying what is missing; the owner reads it.
    Whichever rule gives whom: when the message goes on from one of his posts just before it, give that post's number
    as joins.
    His message may be several posts sent close together, each with its number ("#590: ..."). Read them as one
    message. Only when its posts are for different agents (one about one piece of work, another about another): Split,
    each part the numbers of its posts and the agents it goes to, every post in exactly one part, by the rules above
    for each part. A post marked "his own choice" goes to exactly the agents he chose: its part goes to them and no one
    else, and a post he chose other agents for is in another part.
    The message:
    <<<
    {{ text }}
    >>>
    The agents on the ticket: {{ agents }}
    The ticket's last posts, oldest first:
    {{ recent }}
